      "response": {
        "category": "correctness",
        "consequence": "A failed calendar or video event deletion during reschedule is no longer logged by the existing handler and surfaces as an unhandled rejection. That can crash the process on newer Node versions or fail silently, and the old event may be left on the calendar.",
        "evidence_refs": [
          "source:new:packages/app-store/vital/lib/reschedule.ts@sha256:e38508d7614d5295e4ebdca2b1aeabb21aeb92d4fb966d4bf0754129e4273aca",
          "source:new:packages/app-store/wipemycalother/lib/reschedule.ts@sha256:1b9778c6748fee76741011db5ce900329eb1fcd94579d1855f572fa615a7c97d",
          "source:new:packages/trpc/server/routers/viewer/bookings.tsx@sha256:d9aa8dfd68bde34fc084558df5f77e921b763a0671c7d9de5e3892bf0b25ead4"
        ],
        "explanation": "The callback passed to forEach was changed to async so it can await getCalendar. forEach ignores the returned promise, so the surrounding try/catch (which logs errors) can no longer catch anything thrown inside the callback. This covers getCalendar, calendar.deleteEvent and deleteMeeting, and the same pattern appears in the bookings.tsx requestReschedule handler, which has no try/catch at all. Before the change, a synchronous throw from getCalendar was caught and logged. Now a rejection from getCalendar (for example a failed dynamic import of the app module) or from deleteEvent becomes an unhandled promise rejection. The reschedule flow also continues and returns before the deletions finish.",
        "lines": {
          "end_line": 128,
          "start_line": 125
        },
